1. The vendored Leap V2 platform (MIT)
--------------------------------------

platform/ is a copy of platform sources from 2068-Leap V2 (https://github.com/nchiker/2068-Leap),
release tag v2.0.1-emu.1, commit d19b9ead9880b31c5fee59e71519a42f4c0263b0, MIT License, copyright
(c) 2026 Charlie Day. platform/UPSTREAM.md and platform/MANIFEST record each file and every Leap-C
change to it (deltas D1-D19, as patches in platform/deltas/); tools/check_platform.py verifies them
against the V2 tag. The two parts of V2's platform that V2 had taken from the stock ROM were removed
in 1.0.0-beta.2 (deltas D20 and D21, section 3); the removed text is recorded by its hash only.

The font (platform/kernel/graphics/graphics.asm, FONT_TABLE) is V2's own design, with Leap-C's
glyphs for [ ] { } | ~ ^ \ (delta D8) and for 1 and l (delta D13). It is not taken from the
Spectrum or TS2068 font: no glyph is byte-identical to a stock glyph. Six simple 5-pixel shapes
(a, o, x, y, z and one punctuation mark) equal a stock glyph shifted by one pixel.


2. Tools derived from Leap V2 and ts2068-debug (MIT)
----------------------------------------------------

- tools/lcharness.py is derived from 2068-Leap's tools/qual/qharness.py (tag v2.0.1-emu.1). It
  imports the ZRCP client and the ZEsarUX process control of ts2068-debug
  (https://github.com/nchiker/2068-Debug, MIT License, copyright (c) 2026 Charlie Day) at run time,
  from a separate checkout (CONTRIBUTING.md), and subclasses and adapts them. ts2068-debug is not
  copied into this repository.
- tools/tapes.py is derived from 2068-Leap's tools/tape_synth.py and tools/make_extension_tzx.py.
- tools/sjasm_strict.sh is derived from 2068-Leap's tools/sjasmplus_strict.sh.
- tools/make_dck.sh is derived from 2068-Leap's .dck builder.
- tools/canary.py and tools/check_ram_layout.py follow the ideas of V2's qcanary.py and
  check_ram_layout.py; no code was copied.
- tools/fuse/fuseshim.c is Leap-C's own code. It is loaded into Fuse (GNU GPL v2 or later) by the
  test harness at run time, and declares the layout of one Fuse 1.9.1 structure (memory_page) so
  that it can read Fuse's memory map. It contains no Fuse source code. Fuse is not distributed here.


3. History: stock-ROM-derived code, removed in 1.0.0-beta.2
-----------------------------------------------------------

The TS2068's ROM is Timex's adaptation of the ZX Spectrum ROM, whose code is copyright Amstrad plc
(formerly Sinclair Research Ltd). Leap-C 1.0.0-beta.1 (tag v1.0.0-beta.1) and every earlier build
contained two parts of V2's platform derived from it, which were not offered under the MIT License:

- STORAGE_SEND_BLOCK and STORAGE_RECEIVE_BLOCK in platform/kernel/storage/storage.asm (the tape
  block I/O, 331 bytes of the HOME ROM): V2's port of the stock ROM's SA-BYTES and LD-BYTES;
- the block-graphics quarter generator in GFX_CHAR_TO_FONT_OFFSET (platform/kernel/graphics/
  graphics.asm): two 12-byte sequences equal to the stock ROM's PO-GR-2.

Those images were redistributed under Amstrad's permission to redistribute its Spectrum ROM code
free of charge.

In 1.0.0-beta.2 (Phase 11, M11-tape) both were replaced by Leap-C's own code, written clean-room:
the tape block I/O (src/runtime/tapeblock.asm: TAPE_SEND_BLOCK, TAPE_RECV_BLOCK) by an author who
had only a specification of the tape signal (docs/TAPE_SIGNAL_SPEC.md, itself written from public
descriptions of the cassette format and from Leap-C's own contracts and tests), and the block-
graphics glyph from the screen format alone. docs/TAPE_CLEANROOM.md records who wrote what from
which inputs. V2's versions were deleted from platform/ (deltas D20, D21: platform/deltas/*.cut
record them by hash only). tools/provenance.py compares the production images with the stock 48K
and TS2068 ROMs byte by byte (make check, when the stock ROMs are given): no common run of 12 bytes
or more remains except one, below.

A 15-byte character test in src/shell/highlight.asm, SH_IDCHAR, is byte-identical to the stock
ROM's ALPHA routine. It is Leap-C's own code, written for Leap-C: the usual way to write a range
test for letters on the Z80.

The git history of this repository (commits up to the v1.0.0-beta.1 tag) still contains the derived
code; it remains under the terms stated above for those versions.


4. References used, not included
--------------------------------

Comments in the source cite, without copying them: the "Timex Sinclair 2068 ROM Disassembly"
(David Anderson, 2023) and the annotated ZX Spectrum ROM disassembly at skoolkid.github.io. The
test harnesses compare against stock ROM behaviour in emulators; no stock ROM image is stored here.
